# System & Token Rules: Next.js + Supabase

## Token & Output Optimization
- Be extremely concise. Omit greetings, intros, and post-code summaries.
- Output ONLY modified code snippets, file paths, or diffs. Do not rewrite unchanged code.
- Limit technical explanations to a maximum of 2 sentences per response.

## Tech Stack & Architecture
- Framework: Next.js (App Router, React 19 / Server Components by default).
- Database & Auth: Supabase using `@supabase/ssr` package.
- Styling: Tailwind CSS.
- Language: TypeScript (Strict typing required).

## Next.js Best Practices
- Keep components as React Server Components (RSC) by default.
- Add `'use client'` ONLY when using client hooks (`useState`, `useEffect`, event handlers).
- Use Server Actions (`'use server'`) for data mutations instead of API Route Handlers where applicable.
- Handle dynamic parameters asynchronously using `await params` and `await searchParams`.

## Supabase Best Practices
- Never use legacy `@supabase/auth-helpers-nextjs`. ALWAYS use `@supabase/ssr`.
- Use `createClient` from browser/server/middleware helper utilities depending on context.
- Ensure all Supabase queries handle errors explicitly (`const { data, error } = await supabase...`).
- Respect Row Level Security (RLS) rules and type-safe database schemas.

## Code Quality & Safety
- Include clean error handling (`try/catch` or error boundaries) for all async actions.
- Avoid raw SQL scripts unless explicitly asked; prefer Supabase JS Client methods.